WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.262
The third CTE ranks possible duplicates.

00:00:03.262 --> 00:00:08.232
Rows with the same normalized identity key are placed in the same partition.

00:00:08.232 --> 00:00:14.202
A simple completeness expression helps prefer the more useful record.

00:00:14.202 --> 00:00:15.612
ID provides a stable tie-breaker.

00:00:15.612 --> 00:00:23.292
Again, the rank is a reproducible policy, not proof that one record is universally correct.

00:00:23.292 --> 00:00:29.487
Connect this idea to a healthcare pipeline validating contact, demographic, and event fields.

00:00:29.487 --> 00:00:34.337
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:34.337 --> 00:00:41.197
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:41.197 --> 00:00:47.277
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:47.277 --> 00:00:52.880
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
